sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186371429 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5dfe9dfb-2de9-5049-bb36-b64e2c2a8946" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371429 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186371430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371430 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371430 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371430 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371430 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "280b7aa8-2f12-5405-8f23-6f3ea0f0ea3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371431 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186371431 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371431 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d74d4e6a-a2fb-53e8-8e78-2d14949a12fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371432 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186371432 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 175